 "standard_text": "Review of representation of natural numbers, integers and rational numbers on the number line; rational numbers as recurring/terminating decimals; operations on real numbers; non-recurring/non-terminating decimals and irrational numbers (√2, √3) on the number line; rationalisation of denominators; laws of exponents with integral and rational powers (summary of the CBSE syllabus)",
 "meta_desc": "Class 9 Number Systems worksheet (NCERT Chapter 1): decimal expansions, p/q form, rational and irrational numbers, surds, rationalising denominators and laws of exponents. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Real numbers = rational + irrational",
 "key_sub": "Rational: terminating or recurring decimals. Irrational: never end, never repeat.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How it works</h2><p>A <b>rational number</b> p/q has a terminating or a non-terminating recurring decimal. An <b>irrational number</b> (√2, √3, π) has a non-terminating non-recurring decimal. Together they make the real numbers.</p><p>Identities: (√a + √b)(√a − √b) = a − b, (√a + √b)² = a + b + 2√(ab). To <b>rationalise</b> 1/(a + √b), multiply top and bottom by a − √b.</p><p>Laws of exponents: aᵖ · a<sup>q</sup> = a<sup>p + q</sup>, (aᵖ)<sup>q</sup> = a<sup>pq</sup>, a<sup>−n</sup> = 1/aⁿ, a<sup>m/n</sup> = (ⁿ√a)ᵐ.</p><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Express 0.4<span class='ov'>7</span> in the form p/q.</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x = 0.4777…</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>100x − 10x = 47.7… − 4.7… = 43</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: 43/90</p></div><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Rationalise 1/(7 + 3√2).</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Multiply by 7 − 3√2</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>Denominator 49 − 18 = 31</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (7 − 3√2)/31</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul><li>Writing 0.<span class='ov'>3</span> = 3/10. A recurring decimal needs 9s in the denominator: 3/9 = 1/3.</li><li>Using √a + √b = √(a + b). Only like surds can be added.</li><li>Thinking a<sup>−n</sup> is negative. It is the reciprocal 1/aⁿ.</li></ul>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "How do I convert a recurring decimal to p/q form?",
   "a": "Let x equal the decimal, multiply by 10, 100, … so the repeating part lines up, subtract, and solve for x."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the difference between rational and irrational numbers?",
   "a": "Rational numbers can be written as p/q and have terminating or recurring decimals. Irrational numbers cannot, and their decimals never end or repeat."
  },
  {
   "q": "What does rationalising the denominator mean?",
   "a": "Rewriting a fraction so that the denominator has no square root, by multiplying the numerator and denominator by a suitable surd (the conjugate)."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is 64^(1/3)?",
   "a": "The cube root of 64, which is 4. In general a^(1/n) is the nth root of a."
  }
